\details{
The bipartite representation preserves the full group structure without
projecting to a pairwise network. A group of three members A, B, C
produces a single 3-hyperedge containing all three, not three pairwise
edges AB, AC, BC. This avoids information loss when group interactions are
the primary unit of analysis (Perc et al. 2013).

Unlike \code{\link[=network_hypergraph]{network_hypergraph()}} (which derives hyperedges from a network's
clique structure), \code{group_hypergraph()} takes group memberships
directly. The two functions are complementary:
\itemize{
\item \code{group_hypergraph()} - when group membership is observed
(sessions, transactions, co-authorships).
\item \code{network_hypergraph()} - when only pairwise interactions are
observed and triadic structure must be inferred from triangles.
}

Rows with \code{NA} in the node, hyperedge or weight column are dropped
silently.
}
\note{
Dense and sparse paths are tested for exact equality. The sparse
path is additionally exercised by the full GFCC reproduction from the
Legal Hypergraphs Zenodo archive (3,618 nodes, 46,165 hyperedges and
77,187 nonzero incidences).

A dense incidence with more than \code{.Machine$integer.max} cells cannot be
addressed by the flat cell index, and would exhaust memory well before
that. It raises the classed error \code{hypergraphs_dense_too_large} rather than
attempting the allocation; pass \code{sparse = TRUE} for data at that scale.
}
\section{Frequent sets within groups}{

With \code{group}, \code{top} or \code{min_share}, a data frame is read as transactions.
Each value of \code{hyperedge} (a trial, a session, a basket) is one set, the
distinct \code{node} values it holds, and the sets are counted within each value
of \code{group}, which must be constant within a \code{hyperedge}. The \code{top} most
frequent sets of each value of \code{group} become hyperedges, counted exactly as for clustered sequences below,
and \code{hg_get(hg, what = "sets")} reads them with their \code{count} and \code{share}
of the value's sets. Without \code{group}, the sets are counted over all
hyperedges together, under one value named after \code{hyperedge}
(\code{"All sessions"}).
\code{plot(hg, group = )} draws the sets of one value, every node sized by the
sets that contain it. \code{states} keeps only
the listed \code{node} values, and \code{min_size} the sets of at least that many.
}

\section{Topic combinations}{

Given a mixed-membership topic model fitted by \code{\link[=hg_topics]{hg_topics()}}, every
document is reduced to the set of topics whose share in it is at least
\code{threshold}, the thresholded topic presence used to build topic
co-occurrence networks (Abuhay et al. 2017; Cassi et al. 2017). A
document on love, hate and romance gives the set of those three topics.
The \code{top} most frequent sets become hyperedges, counted exactly as for
clustered sequences below, with each document as one transaction, and the
topics are the nodes. \code{top = Inf} keeps every set, and \code{min_size = 2}
keeps only the sets that combine two or more topics. \code{group} names a column of the documents table of
the fitted hypergraph, such as a publication year or an author, and the
sets are then counted within each of its values; \code{NULL} (default) counts
them over all documents. A document with no topic at the threshold is not
counted. Read the sets with \code{hg_get(hg, what = "sets")}; \code{count} is the
number of documents with exactly that set and \code{share} its proportion of
the group's documents.
}

\section{Clustered sequences}{

Given a clustering of sequences, every sequence of every group is reduced to
the set of its distinct states (order and repetition dropped; \code{NA} and
empty cells ignored), and the \code{top} most frequent sets of each group
become hyperedges -- frequent-itemset support counting with each sequence
as one transaction (Agrawal & Srikant 1994), restricted to the sets that
occur exactly. Sets of equal count are ranked by their name (states
sorted and joined by \code{" + "}). Groups are named \code{"Cluster 1"},
\code{"Cluster 2"}, ... for a \code{net_mmm} or \code{net_clustering}, and by the list
names of a \code{netobject_group}, so renamed groups carry through.
Integer-coded states are decoded to their labels. The objects are read
by their structure; the package that fitted them is not needed.

The hyperedges are named \code{"<group>: <set>"} and carry \code{group}, \code{set} and
\code{count} (sequences with exactly that set) as hyperedge attributes, so
\code{plot()} colours and titles them by \code{count} in \code{"sequences"}, and
\code{plot(hg, group = "Cluster 1")} draws one group's sets with each node
sized by the sequences of that group containing the state. Read the
tables with \code{hg_get(hg, what = "sets")} (one row per hyperedge:
its group, set, size, count and share of the group's sequences) and
\code{hg_get(hg, what = "state_counts")} (one row per group and
state). A malformed clustering (no \verb{$data}, assignments that do not match
it, unnamed networks) raises \code{hypergraphs_bad_input}.

Every other column of \code{data} that is constant within a hyperedge (a
session's date, a team's department) is kept as a hyperedge attribute in
the result's \code{edge_data} table, one row per hyperedge, where
\code{\link[=hg_subset]{hg_subset()}}'s \code{where} argument and \code{\link[=pairwise_network]{pairwise_network()}}'s \code{edge_source} can
use it. Columns that vary within a hyperedge describe memberships, not
hyperedges, and are left out.
}

